g that i've  only ever otherwise gotten from  traveling to foreign countries  the feeling you get from the film isn't  quite as potent but it's impressive that  it even conveys that at all while only  requiring two hours of your time in your  own home versus days and thousands of  dollars in travel  [Music]  now  [Music]  reality tv is a huge part of our media  landscape for better or for worse but  its roots ultimately lie an experimental  documentary when french filmmakers in  the 1950s started to experiment with  pointing cameras at their friends and  everyday people to try to capture  conversation naturally  what they called cinema verite laid the  groundwork for what would inspire shows  like mtv's the real world which in turn  paved the way for much of modern reality  tv  some of the most stunning footage i've  ever seen in any film fiction or  non-fiction  is in werner herzog's lessons of  darkness  the film focuses largely on the kuwait  oil crisis but as if these events on  their own weren't incredible enough  herzog elevates them to an almost cosmic  biblical proportion by surrounding them  in a fictionalized context  in herzog's film he uses images taken  directly from reality but he tells a  story not of an isolated event but of an  apocalypse  such was not since men were upon the  earth so mighty and earthquake and so  great  and the great city was divided into  three parts  and the cities of the nations fell  but does this poetic narrative that  herzog uses to frame this real world  footage make the film a fictional film  does it change the reality of what we're  seeing does it make it not a documentary  herzog doesn't want you to be able to  brush these images aside and honestly  they're difficult images for me to even  grapple with  i mean forget for a moment the fictional  sci-fi dystopias from the far future or  the alternate universe grungy versions  of modern cities that make up most of  our blockbuster films and try to wrap  your head around the