rfall  sleep has her house is one of those  films that really bends our idea of what  can constitute a documentary and even a  movie  by all means it doesn't seem like it  would be a documentary but it's been  classed as and won awards as a  documentary in film festivals and you  can certainly make the case for it as a  documentary of sorts it's mostly images  of nature it's a mood piece that relies  on natural imagery and so where is the  fiction really  what part of this is unreal  you might be wondering why anyone would  watch something like this and i would  classify a film like this as something  closer to a sort of guided meditation  rather than a traditional narrative  experience and i think there's a  definite value in that kind of  experience  there are very few times outside of  movies where i've sat in silence and  simply absorbed images of nature for  over an hour uninterrupted and if you  allow it to be that can be a profound  and interesting experience especially  when contrasted against the pace  and hyperactivity of most of what we  consume these days  there's a feeling this film gave me that  i've only ever had in one other scenario  one where i found myself sitting  stranded alongside a river in the winter  with a wrecked canoe and wet gear  sitting by a fire trying to stay warm as  night was falling and i was waiting for  help  some of this will probably sound  pretentious if you're not into this kind  of thing but people who are trying to  find the extreme boundaries of what a  medium is capable of i think needs some  kind of pretense to their work  it's them imagining that the form can do  something more than what it's normally  used for they're testing it and breaking  it and discovering new interesting  territory in the medium some of us might  enjoy going on that journey with these  artists and some of us might not  but all of us have benefited from the  pioneers at the fringes who have often  been the ones discovering the techniques  that then work their way into mainstream  filmmaking as part of the everyday  narrative toolkit that audiences come to  accept  much of this has very deep roots in film  history in the 1920s soviet filmmakers  were placing a lot of emphasis on the  simple power of juxtaposing images as a  means of communicating ideas filmmakers  like sergei eisenstein saw the primary  language of film as a poetic and visual  one  constructed through the connection of  images and editing and i think  filmmaking as a form has yet to realize  the full potential of what he envisioned  for it man was a movie camera is an  experimental doc directed by zika vertov  where you can clearly see how the story  of a community can be constructed  through image alone the film has a  direct connection to films like koyana  scotsie and i think it's these strange  kind of non-verbal documentaries that  are often devoid of traditional  narrative that can help us really  understand the power that images alone  have to convey ideas and when we  understand that power we understand the  full depth that cinema can offer when  words and dialogue are added it makes  sense to me that i love these kinds of  films in a way video essays are just an  extension of the documentary tradition  and for documentaries which are usually  shot without scripts the editor gains an  even greater significance as a  storyteller in shaping and crafting the  story from mountains of gathered footage  documentary is less of a genre and more  of a creative practice and approach to  the form  one where more often than not the story  must be discovered instead of created  for me these films reveal the power  available to filmmakers as storytellers  simply through removing or changing the  context surrounding an image of crafting  a narrative simply by connecting images  and editing  and the possibility  of pace as a means of meditation or  communication  these are the films that can tell  stories that are global or cosmic in  scale that del